{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =-2t{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =-2t{e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =-2(t-1)+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+2-2t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=2-2t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8fa9ad0a8cff17849e9ab05de6505d8b5aaaea7dd244149dcda3184bfb7393e"} {"question" "q-337103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(t)=1-2t"] ["math" "f(t)=2+2t"] ["math" "f(t)=2+t"] ["math" "f(t)=2-2t"] ["math" "f(t)=2-t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(t)=2-2t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then the general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{3(1-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{3(1-t)}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{3(1-t)}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{3}{t}-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+3t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}+3t.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=3t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+3t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8fa9ad0a8cff17849e9ab05de6505d8b5aaaea7dd244149dcda3184bfb7393e"} {"question" "q-337713" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337713" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{t}^{2}+3"] ["math" "3-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-2t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}+3t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-3t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-337713" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{t}^{2}+3t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2}) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2}) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-2t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}-2t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt-2t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-2t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt-2t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt-2t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt-2t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt-2t \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =-2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{-2t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{-2}{1-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{2}{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8fa9ad0a8cff17849e9ab05de6505d8b5aaaea7dd244149dcda3184bfb7393e"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{t-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{2t}{t-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{1-t}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{t-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{t-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{-2t}{t-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{2t}{1-t}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{1-t}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{t-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365377" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{t-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{t} & =-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t} & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{{t}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={t}^{2}-x \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8fa9ad0a8cff17849e9ab05de6505d8b5aaaea7dd244149dcda3184bfb7393e"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365387" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=3xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+7{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=3xcos\\,(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+7{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+7{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+7{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+3x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+14t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+14t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =(14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} \\\\ (t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =14t-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =14t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{14t}{t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8fa9ad0a8cff17849e9ab05de6505d8b5aaaea7dd244149dcda3184bfb7393e"} {"question" "q-365388" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073105/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e6b3f60c63697321adf107027986c9265904a8db05402e28e9228e823d4648c"} {"question" "q-365388" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "